still smoking the chemical fire on board  the stricken vessel  is now out but too late to avert an  unfolding environmental disaster  the nitric acid which was being  transported on the express pearl  is thought to have burnt off but with  the cargo ship  slowly sinking a last glimpse of some of  its  1500 containers it had been carrying  plastic pellets used to make things like  plastic bags millions of the pellets  have washed up on sri lanka's pristine  beaches  presenting environmentalists with a  plastic time bomb  already they're documenting the feared  impact on marine life  like this dead turtle it's pretty sad to  see  i visited one of the beaches uh today  the one that's  sort of directly opposite where this  ship caught fire  where all like a bulk of the pellets  have been  uh collecting and it's not a pretty  sight it used to be this beautiful  tropical beach with coconut trees along  the side  very idyllic and today when i went there  i was i was very heartbroken to see  um piles of plastic pellets everywhere  efforts to tow the cargo ship further  out to sea to dilute the environmental  damage it's  causing have failed it is just nine  nautical miles from the port of colombo  fishermen who've been ordered to stay in  port are angry that their livelihoods  hang in the balance  the ship has dealt a fatal blow to our  lives we can't go to the sea which means  we can't make a living  the indian and sri lankan navies have  been working together for several weeks  to try to stop the ship from sinking  it's not yet clear whether a major oil  spill has been averted  but it's feared the spillage of plastic  pellets could get  carried to beaches as far away as  indonesia  a distance of 4 000 kilometers  lisa holland sky news
 

